Step 1: Extracting the Sender and Recipient Information
The raw email headers were inspected to determine both the source and the intended recipient of the email. Within the headers:

The From: field revealed the sender's email as amazon@zyevantoby.cn, an untrusted and suspicious domain.

The To: field listed the recipient as saintington73@outlook.com, indicating the targeted user.

Step 2: Identifying the Email Subject
Within the header section, the Subject: line was clearly stated as:

Your Account has been locked

This is a typical subject used to create urgency and trick users into taking immediate action.

Step 3: Verifying the Impersonated Brand
Through both the From: display name (“Amazn”) and the content of the email, it was evident the attacker was impersonating Amazon. The style, tone, and references to account access and payment further supported this.

Step 4: Capturing the Sent Timestamp
Using the Date: header:

Wed, 14 Jul 2021 01:40:32 +0900

This gave an exact timestamp of when the phishing email was dispatched.

Step 5: Extracting the Call-to-Action Link
Within the HTML portion of the email, a "Review Account" button was discovered. Upon examining the encoded base64 content and decoding it, the CTA URL was identified as a long Microsoft SafeLink redirect which led to a suspicious external URL — a common phishing tactic.

Step 6: Reviewing the Linked Page
Using the decoded link and a preview tool like URL2PNG, the landing page displayed the heading:

Hello Dear Customer, Your account access has been limited.

This confirmed the phishing narrative of account compromise.

Step 7: Identifying Encoding Scheme
The email body used the following header:

Content-Transfer-Encoding: base64

This confirmed the use of base64 encoding for obfuscating malicious HTML.

Step 8: Detecting Embedded Media
An <img src="..."> tag in the HTML body pointed to a logo hosted externally, pretending to be an Amazon asset. The logo was sourced from a CDN URL, not from Amazon’s official domain.

Step 9: Discovering a Hidden Facebook Profile Link
While reviewing all hyperlinks, a Facebook redirect was found within one of the encoded <a href="..."> tags. After decoding the URL, the following user profile was uncovered:

https://www.facebook.com/amir.boyka.7

The username amir.boyka.7 was extracted from this.

Conclusion
This phishing email leveraged brand impersonation, urgency-based language, hidden redirect links, and base64-encoded HTML to disguise malicious content. It serves as a valuable example for phishing awareness and forensics practice.
